      "depth": "moderate",
      "if_shallow": "Anchor the chill claim on the vaccine and general-aviation precedents and on the distributional point the topic itself raises — 'the power disparity between tech giants and individuals' — arguing that unbounded exposure differentially eliminates small entrants, per sit-166 and sit-278, rather than asserting a measured AI deployment slowdown."
    },
    {
      "angle": "Hardware-level telemetry and cryptographic audit trails as the compliance gate",
      "grounding_evidence": "Existing building blocks — trusted platform modules, confidential-computing attestation on current accelerators, transparency-log constructions (certificate-transparency-style append-only Merkle logs), and financial-sector immutable audit-trail requirements (e.g., consolidated audit trail regimes). No deployed regulatory regime yet conditions liability coverage on attested runtime telemetry at model scale.",
      "depth": "shallow",
      "if_shallow": "Reframe as the program's central design question the debate should resolve: what telemetry is attestable at the accelerator level without exposing model_weights or trade secrets, what retention and subpoena-access rules apply, and what happens when logs are incomplete — with a fallback rule that unverifiable compliance defaults to the uncovered tier and full exposure. Do not claim the technology is turnkey."
    },
    {
      "angle": "Federal uniformity is a functional precondition of the immunity/compensation trade",
      "grounding_evidence": "VICP's preemption architecture and Bruesewitz; Price-Anderson's exclusive-jurisdiction provisions; ERISA and FIFRA preemption as models for channeling. Counter-precedent is strong and must be conceded: California vehicle-emissions waivers show state experimentation improving national standards, and the EU AI Act shows purpose-built statutes can impose heavier burdens than adapted doctrine.",
      "depth": "moderate",
      "if_shallow": "Limit the preemption claim to the compensation channel itself — claims for covered injuries in covered tiers route through the federal board — while leaving state authority intact for non-compliers and for domains outside the tier schedule. This is a floor-setting design, not a ceiling, and should be argued as such."
    },
    {
      "angle": "Excise tax as harm internalization funding safety research, auditing, and compensation",
      "grounding_evidence": "VICP's excise-funded Vaccine Injury Compensation Trust Fund is a working precedent for hypothecated, per-unit funding of a claims program; Black Lung Disability Trust Fund and the Oil Spill Liability Trust Fund are further hypothecated-levy analogues. Uncertain in the AI case: the tax base definition (per query, per compute-hour, per revenue) and the actuarial claim-frequency estimate needed to size the fund.",
      "depth": "moderate",
      "if_shallow": "Concede that fund sizing is an actuarial unknown and propose an explicit solvency trigger: if claims exceed reserves, the tier rate adjusts automatically and coverage caps do not silently shift losses onto claimants — otherwise the immunity becomes uncompensated."
    }
  ],
  "key_tensions": [
    {
      "tension": "Granting immunity while claiming to increase accountability — the opposition will call this an industry shield dressed as victim protection (sit-166, sit-070).",
      "opportunity": "Invert the burden: ask what percentage of AI-injured claimants currently recover anything through tort given multi-agent attribution failure and judgment-proof deployers (acc-beliefs-093). Frame the status quo as the real shield, and the no-fault board as the first mechanism that pays claimants without proving causation."
