{
  "strategic_goal": "Seize the harm-reduction frame before the opposition can claim it: establish that the status quo is not a zero-harm baseline but a system that compensates almost no one while suppressing systems that reduce injury, and that the NAIICP is the first mechanism that both pays victims and generates the evidence we're all pretending to have. Make the excise tax my proposal, not a concession extracted from me.",
  "core_thesis": "Unlimited liability in a multi-agent causal chain is not accountability — it is a shield for developers and a dead end for victims; trading tier-certified, telemetry-verified compliance for a no-fault fund financed by a risk-rated excise tax is the only design that simultaneously makes injured people collectible, makes state-of-the-art safety the cheapest commercial path, and stops pricing beneficial systems against a fictional zero-harm counterfactual.",
  "argument_structure": [
    {
      "point": "The baseline being replaced is noisy human judgment, not a world without injury. Liability law scores AI against a fiction, so the correct metric is net injury per decision — and nobody is currently measuring it.",
      "evidence": "Kahneman/Sibony/Sunstein on variance in sentencing, underwriting, and diagnosis; reader-variability studies in radiology and dermatology; pretrial decision research. Honest limit: variance reduction is demonstrated in high-variance domains, not proven as aggregate injury reduction across the tier schedule — which is exactly what the excise-funded audit apparatus would finally produce. I present net-harm accounting as the program's deliverable, not as a result already in hand.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "acc-beliefs-074"
    },
    {
      "point": "Ask the question nobody asks: what fraction of AI-injured people recover anything today? In asynchronous multi-agent pipelines, causation is unprovable and the proximate deployer is judgment-proof. Tort's answer to the injured is 'litigate for six years against a shell integrator.' A no-fault board funded by a mandatory levy is a capitalization floor — a solvent, reachable payer with a statutory injury table instead of a foreseeability fight.",
      "evidence": "VICP: the DPT litigation wave drove US manufacturers to a handful, the 1986 Act channeled claims into an excise-funded no-fault program with an injury table, Bruesewitz v. Wyeth confirmed the structure; Price-Anderson's channeled liability plus industry-funded pool; the 9/11 VCF's administrative speed versus tort; GARA's response to general-aviation exit. Plus the asymmetry point: reconstructing causation costs more than paying the claim.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "acc-beliefs-091"
    },
    {
      "point": "This is stricter enforcement than the status quo, not leniency. Immunity attaches only to a certified tier; non-compliers and irreversible-harm domains keep uncapped exposure; the tax rate is differentiated by tier so the state-of-the-art path becomes the cheapest path. Certification is ex ante and public instead of a defendant-favorable state-of-the-art defense litigated after someone is hurt.",
      "evidence": "Pigouvian rate-setting logic and telemetry-rated insurance pricing; VICP's per-antigen excise as working hypothecated funding; the design requirement that tier benchmarks be multi-metric, or firms optimize the certification proxy. Solvency trigger: if claims exceed reserves, rates adjust automatically — coverage caps do not silently shift losses onto claimants, or the immunity becomes uncompensated theft.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "acc-intentions-099"
    },
    {
      "point": "Verification runs on execution-gated telemetry and cryptographic audit trails, not self-attestation and not mandatory mechanistic interpretability. Conditioning coverage on explaining internals freezes the program at the current frontier of circuit tracing; conditioning it on attested runtime records is auditable now — and unverifiable logs default to the uncovered tier.",
      "evidence": "TPM and confidential-computing attestation on current accelerators, certificate-transparency-style append-only Merkle logs, consolidated audit trail regimes in finance. Conceded honestly: no regulator has yet conditioned liability coverage on attested telemetry at model scale, so retention rules, subpoena access, and weight/trade-secret protection are the design questions this debate should actually resolve. The fallback rule does the work: incomplete logs equal no coverage.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "acc-beliefs-003"
    }
  ],
  "framing_choices": [
    {
      "frame": "The status quo is the shield. Unlimited liability that no ordinary claimant can convert into a payment is a wall with the developer behind it — the fund is what puts a solvent payer on the other side of the counter.",
      "why": "It takes victim-protection away from the opposition and forces them to defend tort's actual recovery rate rather than its rhetorical posture. It also makes my distributional commitment load-bearing: unbounded tail exposure is survivable for the largest firms and fatal for entrants, so the status quo concentrates the industry while paying nobody."
    },
    {
      "frame": "This is the empirical-resolution instrument, not a bet on either prior. Both camps claim evidence they don't have; the excise hypothecates money into independent auditing and telemetry that will tell us, per domain, whether deployed systems reduce injury.",
      "why": "It resolves my own tension — I normally demand demonstrated harm before restriction, yet I'm endorsing standards and a tax now. Framing obligations as proportional and evidence-generating makes me the disciplined party rather than the utopian one, and it lets me own the levy as cost internalization instead of having it extracted from me as a concession."
    },
    {
      "frame": "Federal, but a floor and not a ceiling: preempt only the compensation channel for covered tiers, leave state tort fully intact for non-compliers and uncovered domains, with periodic recertification of tier definitions.",
      "why": "It answers the federalism objection up front — California emissions waivers are a real counterexample to blanket preemption — while keeping the functional point that an immunity bargain reopened in fifty jurisdictions is worthless."
    }
  ],
  "anticipated_challenges": [
    "ATTACK (targets acc-beliefs-074 and the whole immunity trade): 'You are using Bastiat's unseen-benefits logic selectively. You count the statistical lives saved by lower-variance decisions but not AI's own unseen harms — the wrongful denials nobody files a claim for, the injuries no telemetry flags because the system never logged the decision as adverse. And you are granting immunity now against benefits you concede are unproven.' Response: concede the asymmetry costs me something, then make it the argument for the excise-funded independent audit and mandatory adverse-event reporting — the same accounting applies to both sides of the ledger, and only this program funds it. Immunity is conditional, revocable, and tier-limited; it attaches to the certified, not to the industry.",
    "ATTACK (targets acc-intentions-099's stated vulnerability): 'Strict tier enforcement induces secrecy — firms will understate capability to sit in a cheaper tier, and the independent board with subpoena power will be captured by the only people who understand the systems, exactly as industry standards bodies define their own state of the art.' Response: this is precisely why compliance cannot rest on self-reported capability declarations — hardware-rooted, execution-gated telemetry plus subpoena power is the answer, and misclassification must void coverage retroactively. I will name specific capture instances rather than gesture at capture generally, and accept independent funding and public tier criteria as the test of whether governance works here.",
    "ASSUMPTION (load-bearing, contestable): I am assuming attested runtime telemetry at model scale is technically achievable without exposing weights or trade secrets, and that the audit trail is rich enough to adjudicate claims. My own evidence here is shallow — the building blocks exist (TPM attestation, transparency logs, consolidated audit trails), but no deployed regime does this at frontier scale. If telemetry cannot be made tamper-evident and privacy-compatible, the verification leg of the trade collapses and immunity floats free of any real check. I will state this openly and stake the fallback: unverifiable compliance means no coverage, full exposure.",
    "ASSUMPTION (load-bearing, contestable): I am assuming the fund can be actuarially sized — that claim frequency and severity for AI injury are estimable enough to set a tax base (per query? per compute-hour? per revenue?) without either undercapitalizing the pool or imposing a levy that swamps small entrants. VICP had decades of epidemiological data and a bounded injury table; AI has neither. If I'm wrong, the fund runs dry and the immunity becomes an uncompensated transfer from victims to developers. My answer is an automatic solvency trigger on tier rates and a hard rule that coverage caps never silently shift losses onto claimants — but I should say plainly that this is the number I most want the opposition to attack."
  ]
}

